From 3f68ab3ec6e9f59a42cf236ec25383584a6ca2b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 03:40:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/19] wifi: rtl8192f-soc: switch the PHY between 20 and 40 MHz

The driver negotiated 20 MHz only: ht_cap lacked SUP_WIDTH_20_40, so
cfg80211_chandef_usable() rejected any 40 MHz chandef and the whole
bandwidth path stayed unwritten.  Port the vendor's switch, which is
SwBWMode88XX_97F() plus the BB half of
config_phydm_switch_bandwidth_8197f(), and follow it through the rest of
the TX chain, because moving the analyser is not enough - the PHY takes
the width per descriptor:

 - REG_DATA_SC (0x483) names the secondary channel, the BB "RF mode"
   pair 0x800[0]/0x900[0] selects the width, the DAC clock (0x800[14:12])
   and ADC buffer clock (0xca4[27:26]) carry the same value on both
   widths in the vendor's two branches, and the ADC clock (0xbb0[18:16])
   is only programmed on the way back to 20 MHz because the 40 MHz
   branch leaves it to the BB init table.  RF 0x18[11:10] is merged into
   the channel write so the synthesizer and the width change together.
 - TX descriptor W5 DATA_BW/DATA_SC say how wide each frame is (vendor
   8192cd_tx.c 3435-3452 for HT rates, 3608-3620 for narrower ones), and
   the resident beacon slot gets the same secondary-channel code
   (FillBeaconDesc88XX_V1, Hal88XXTxDesc.c 3138-3145).  Note that this
   encoding is the mirror image of the REG_DATA_SC one.
 - The RA_MASK H2C carries the width in byte 2 and picks the short-GI
   capability for that width in byte 7 (H2CCommand[1]/[2] in
   Hal88XXFirmware.c 1552-1555, Get_RA_ShortGI88XX :1098-1111).
 - The PA ladder changes with the width: at 40 MHz the HT group uses the
   stored HT40 level directly and only the OFDM group takes its
   per-channel difference (Hal88XXPhyCfg.c 4512-4516).
 - ht_cap advertises SHORT-GI-40 as the shipped AP does (the mib default
   dot11nShortGIfor40M is 1, 8192cd_net80211.c:245), and the RX width
   now comes out of the type-1 PHY status rather than being assumed,
   since the 8197F RX info descriptor has no bandwidth bit
   (phydm_hwconfig.c 3270-3289).

Channel-edge widths need no driver flags: cfg80211_chandef_valid()
rejects any non-20_NOHT chandef on channel 14 and
cfg80211_chandef_usable() requires every sub-channel of the chandef to
be one this driver declared, which is also how rtw88 leaves its table.

Signed-off-by: David Yang <mmyangfl@gmail.com>
